    "title": "Orange 13 Listening - Test 2 - Listening Passage 4",
    "status": "published",
    "time": 11,
    "quiz_code": "",
    "parts": [
      {
        "id": 6464,
        "quiz_id": 6375,
        "passage": 4,
        "title": "Listening Passage 4",
        "sort": 4,
        "content": "",
        "explanations": [],
        "questions": [],
        "vocabs": [
          {
            "id": 840307,
            "level": 1,
            "value": "",
            "children": [
              {
                "id": 840308,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "Today, we’ll be continuing the series of lectures on memory by focusing on what is called episodic memory and what can happen if this is not working properly.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 92.95,
                  "from": 79.37,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": 840337,
            "level": 1,
            "value": "",
            "children": [
              {
                "id": 840338,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "Episodic memory refers to the memory of an event or ’episode’.",
                "youpass_pick_words": [
                  "episode"
                ],
                "meta": {
                  "to": 99.59,
                  "from": 94.16,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 840349,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "Episodic memories allow us to mentally travel back in time to an event from the past.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 107.7,
                  "from": 100.64,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 840366,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "Episodic memories include various details about these events, for example, when an event happened and other information such as the location.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 121.75,
                  "from": 108.58,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 840387,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "To help understand this concept, try to remember the last time you ate dinner at a restaurant.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 130.24,
                  "from": 123.36,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 840404,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "The ability to remember where you ate, who you were with and the items you ordered are all features of an episodic memory.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 141.84,
                  "from": 131.52,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": 840427,
            "level": 1,
            "value": "",
            "children": [
              {
                "id": 840428,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "Episodic memory is distinct from another type of memory called semantic memory.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 150.74,
                  "from": 143.45,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 840441,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "This is the type of factual memory that we have in common with everyone else -- that is your general knowledge of the world.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 162.12,
                  "from": 151.53,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 840466,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "To build upon a previous example, remembering where you parked your car is an example of episodic memory, but your understanding of what a car is and how an engine works are examples of sematic memory.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 183.0,
                  "from": 163.4,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 840501,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "Unlike episodic memory, semantic memory isn’t dependent on recalling personal experiences.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 192.71,
                  "from": 183.79,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": 840515,
            "level": 1,
            "value": "",
            "children": [
              {
                "id": 840516,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "Episodic memory can be thought of as a process with several different steps of memory processing: encoding, consolidation and retrieval.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 208.72,
                  "from": 194.55,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": 840536,
            "level": 1,
            "value": "",
            "children": [
              {
                "id": 840537,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "The initial step is called encoding.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 212.95,
                  "from": 209.91,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 840544,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "This involves the process of receiving and registering information, which is necessary for creating memories of information or events that you experience.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 226.46,
                  "from": 214.38,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 840566,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "The degree to which you can successfully encode information depends on the level of attention you give to an event while it’s actually happening.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 239.71,
                  "from": 227.87,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 840591,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "Being distracted can make effective encoding very difficult.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 246.46,
                  "from": 241.02,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 840600,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "Encoding of episodic memories is also influenced by how you process the event.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 254.86,
                  "from": 247.5,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 840614,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "For example, if you were introduced to someone called Charlie, you might make the connection that your uncle has the same name.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 266.62,
                  "from": 255.91,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 840637,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "Future recollection of Charlie’s name is much easier if you have a strategy to help you encode it.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 275.96,
                  "from": 267.5,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": 840655,
            "level": 1,
            "value": "",
            "children": [
              {
                "id": 840656,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "Memory consolidation, the next step in forming an episodic memory, is the process by which memories of encoded information are strengthened, stabilised and stored to facilitate later retrieval.",
                "youpass_pick_words": [
                  "facilitate"
                ],
                "meta": {
                  "to": 295.55,
                  "from": 277.88,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 840685,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "Consolidation is most effective when the information being stored can be linked to an existing network of information.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 307.82,
                  "from": 296.99,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 840704,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "Consolidation makes it possible for you to store memories for later retrieval indefinitely.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 316.56,
                  "from": 309.42,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 840718,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "Forming strong memories depends on the frequency with which you try to retrieve them.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 325.51,
                  "from": 317.75,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 840733,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "Memories can fade or become harder to retrieve if they aren’t used very often.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 333.27,
                  "from": 326.47,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": 840747,
            "level": 1,
            "value": "",
            "children": [
              {
                "id": 840748,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "The last step in forming episodic memories is called retrieval, which is the conscious recollection of encoded information.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 345.94,
                  "from": 334.71,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 840767,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "Retrieving information from episodic memory depends upon semantic, olfactory, auditory and visual factors.",
                "youpass_pick_words": [
                  "auditory"
                ],
                "meta": {
                  "to": 358.54,
                  "from": 347.06,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 840781,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "These help episodic memory retrieval by acting as a prompt.",
                "youpass_pick_words": [
                  "prompt"
                ],
                "meta": {
                  "to": 365.74,
                  "from": 359.66,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 840791,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "For example, when recalling where you parked your car you may use the colour of a sign close to where you parked.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 376.86,
                  "from": 366.54,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 840813,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "You actually have to mentally travel back to the moment you parked.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 382.86,
                  "from": 377.65,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": 840826,
            "level": 1,
            "value": "",
            "children": [
              {
                "id": 840827,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "There are a wide range of neurological diseases and conditions that can affect episodic memory.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 397.19,
                  "from": 388.39,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 840842,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "These range from Alzheimer’s to schizophrenia to autism.",
                "youpass_pick_words": [
                  "autism"
                ],
                "meta": {
                  "to": 404.4,
                  "from": 398.17,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 840851,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "An impairment of episodic memory can have a profound effect on individuals’ lives.",
                "youpass_pick_words": [
                  "profound"
                ],
                "meta": {
                  "to": 413.52,
                  "from": 405.69,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 840864,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "For example, the symptoms of schizophrenia can be reasonably well controlled by medication; however, patients’ episodic memory may still be impaired and so they are often unable to return to university or work.",
                "youpass_pick_words": [
                  "impaired"
                ],
                "meta": {
                  "to": 435.19,
                  "from": 414.54,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 840898,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "Recent studies have shown that computer- assisted games designed to keep the brain active can help improve their episodic memory.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 447.45,
                  "from": 436.31,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": 840918,
            "level": 1,
            "value": "",
            "children": [
              {
                "id": 840919,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "Episodic memories can help people connect with others, for instance by sharing intimate details about their past; something individuals with autism often have problems with.",
                "youpass_pick_words": [
                  "intimate"
                ],
                "meta": {
                  "to": 465.6,
                  "from": 449.45,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 840945,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "This may be caused by an absence of a sense of self.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 471.89,
                  "from": 466.4,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 840958,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "This is essential for the storage of episodic memory, and has been found to be impaired in children with autism.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 482.85,
                  "from": 473.17,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 840980,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "Research has shown that treatments that improve memory may also have a positive impact on children’s social development.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 495.12,
                  "from": 484.4,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": 840998,
            "level": 1,
            "value": "",
            "children": [
              {
                "id": 840999,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "One study looked at a ...",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 499.12,
                  "from": 496.64,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              }
            ]
          }
        ],
